      reportFooter: 'CT and clinical criteria assessed per ATS/ERS 2020-2025 consensus guidelines. This report is a reference aid and does not replace clinical judgment or multidisciplinary discussion.',
      usage: [
        'Stage 1 (ILA vs. ILD?): follows the ATS 2025 consensus statement on Interstitial Lung Abnormalities (ILA). A case is classified as frank ILD when there are attributable symptoms or abnormal pulmonary function, ≥5% fibrotic extent of total lung volume, radiologic progression on serial CT, or a major fibrotic pattern (UIP/probable UIP, fibrotic HP, or fibrotic NSIP) — Table 3, ATS 2025. If these criteria are not met but ≥5% involvement of at least one lung zone is present, the case is classified as ILA (Table 1, ATS 2025).',
        'Stage 2 (pattern characterization): the typical UIP / probable / indeterminate / alternative-diagnosis-suggestive classification, and the suggested alternative diagnoses, are only fully displayed once frank ILD is confirmed; they follow the joint ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT guideline criteria for IPF/progressive pulmonary fibrosis and the unified ERS/ATS 2025 nomenclature.',
        'Signs suggestive of connective tissue disease (straight-edge sign, exuberant honeycombing, anterior upper lobe sign, esophageal dilatation, axillary lymphadenopathy — Chung et al. 2018; Marinescu et al. 2026) favor an alternative diagnosis to IPF even in the presence of a morphologically UIP pattern.',
        'Smoking-related entities (SRIF, PLCH, RB-ILD) can now appear as the name of the main result (not only as a note inside the suggestions box) when they are the reason for a pattern inconsistent with UIP, in order of diagnostic specificity of the findings present. SRIF and RB-ILD are only named as such when there is a smoking history; without smoking, the same findings (irregular cysts, or centrilobular ground-glass nodules) are not attributed to those entities.',
        'Small airway disease signs (mosaic attenuation/three-density pattern, peribronchovascular distribution, or centrilobular nodules without a smoking history) make the headline read "Pattern Inconsistent with UIP, with features suggestive of BIP" — the breakdown into Fibrotic vs. Non-Fibrotic Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis, and whether the pattern is typical (diffuse distribution) or compatible/variant (Tables 5 and 6, ATS 2020), is detailed in the suggestions box, not the headline.',
        'An alternative clinical criterion for frank ILD was added ("major interstitial disease"): it allows classifying as ILD by magnitude/extent even when the pattern is not fibrotic (e.g., extensive or diffuse ground-glass opacity), bypassing the Table 3 imaging criterion (which requires a fibrotic pattern ≥5% of total lung volume). Left to the radiologist\'s judgment.',
        'Suspicion of Organizing Pneumonia (OP) is now also triggered by peribronchovascular- or perilobular-distribution (arcade-like) consolidations, in addition to patchy consolidations/reversed halo sign.',
        'Extensive ground-glass opacity, or a pattern whose predominant feature is already ground-glass opacity, is now highlighted prominently in the result box (not only as a secondary note): in both cases it is preferable not to classify definitively and instead document persistence or obtain a follow-up CT at 3-6 months, to rule out a superimposed infectious or acute exacerbation component.',
        'When suspicion of connective tissue disease arises only from morphologic findings (no known diagnosis selected), the result explicitly names the sign(s) present ("...; the association with [signs] raises the possibility of an underlying connective tissue disease") instead of directly stating "CTD-associated ILD" — that affirmative phrasing is reserved for when a known, selected history of connective tissue disease is present.',
        'When a known history of connective tissue disease is indicated (SLE, Sjögren, systemic sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, polymyositis/dermatomyositis, or MCTD), the most frequently associated ILD pattern is suggested per Ahuja et al. 2016, and the result is flagged as consistent with CTD-associated ILD.',
        'This tool is an educational aid for structuring the report and does NOT replace multidisciplinary discussion (MDD), the reference standard for the definitive diagnosis of IPF and other fibrosing ILDs.',
      ],
