      usage: [
        'Fat fraction (%) = (In-phase signal − Out-of-phase signal) / (2 × In-phase signal) × 100. Based on fat/water signal cancellation on chemical-shift (Dixon) sequences. Useful when both echoes are acquired within a single excitation (simple dual-echo).',
        'Alternative method — spleen-normalized fat percentage (Ma et al., RadioGraphics 2009): Percentage (%) = 100 × [(Liver SI-IP/Spleen SI-IP) − (Liver SI-OP/Spleen SI-OP)] / (2 × Liver SI-IP/Spleen SI-IP). Normalizing hepatic signal against splenic signal at each phase compensates for T2* differences between acquisitions when the in-phase and out-of-phase images are obtained on separate sequences/excitations (not a single dual-echo acquisition); the spleen is used as a fat-free reference tissue.',
        'Both formulas are useful for quantifying hepatic steatosis (>5-6% suggests steatosis) and for characterizing lesions with microscopic fat content (e.g., adrenal adenoma, hepatocellular adenoma).',
        'Rough categorization of the estimated fat percentage: <5% normal; 5-14.9% mild steatosis; 15-29.9% moderate steatosis; ≥30% marked steatosis.',
        'Limitation: the simple dual-echo technique (either method) overestimates fat in livers with high fat content due to T2* effects and is less accurate than multi-echo proton density fat fraction (PDFF).',
      ],
