      specificManagementBSymptomsNote: 'With B symptoms or suspected systemic malignancy, staging with whole-body CT, PET-CT, or hematology/oncology referral for targeted biopsy is recommended, rather than routine imaging follow-up.',
      specificManagementMassEffectNote: 'Due to local mass effect, hemorrhage, or suspected/impending rupture, urgent evaluation by surgery or interventional radiology is recommended.',
      specificManagementHypersplenismNote: 'With cytopenias from hypersplenism, hematology evaluation and correlation for portal hypertension or other myelo/lymphoproliferative disorders are recommended.',
      suspiciousVerdictBig: 'Suspicious features',
      suspiciousNote: 'Heterogeneous structure or associated splenomegaly without a typically benign finding to explain them, irregular margins, parenchymal/vascular invasion, necrosis, or demonstrated growth on prior follow-up correspond to suspicious features (ACR 2013 flowchart / Dr. Hevia\'s teaching algorithm), which recommend further evaluation (MRI, PET-CT, or biopsy/splenectomy) rather than follow-up.',
      benignOrStableVerdictBig: 'Benign or stable lesion',
      benignOrStableNoMalignancyNote: 'The imaging pattern is characteristic of a benign entity (typical cyst, hemangioma, SANT, hamartoma, littoral cell angioma) or the lesion has been demonstrated stable on prior follow-up for ≥1 year — with no history of malignancy and no symptoms. Clinical or imaging follow-up (CT/MRI) at 6-12 months is suggested; additional evidence (Siewert et al., Radiology 2018 — 205 truly incidental patients, 1.0% malignancy) suggests that even this follow-up may be omitted in this specific setting, based on clinical judgment.',
      benignOrStableKnownMalignancyNote: 'The imaging pattern is characteristic of a benign entity, or the lesion has been demonstrated stable on prior follow-up for ≥1 year; however, given the history of extra-splenic malignancy, clinical correlation is recommended. Per Heller et al. (ACR 2013), a lesion >1 cm in this setting should raise suspicion for metastasis unless the pattern is diagnostic of a clearly benign entity (e.g., a typical simple cyst); a lesion <1 cm is likely benign even in this setting.',
